source_comment_body:
This synchronization is racy, in both directions. The `"error"` message that triggers the fix travels over the *transferred* MessagePort, while `"done"` travels over the worker's global channel — two separate channels with no ordering guarantee between them. The main thread can resolve, terminate the worker, and end the test before it has processed the `"error"` message and closed the port, so this can fail spuriously after the fix (and pass spuriously without it).

Suggest synchronizing on something observable on the main thread instead. The cancel reason propagates back through `pipeTo` to the original underlying source, and `pipeTo` only cancels the source *after* the `"error"` handler has run (and thus after `port.close()`), so this is deterministic and gives the test a real assertion rather than relying solely on the sanitizer:

```ts
const { promise: cancelled, resolve: onCancel } = Promise
  .withResolvers<string>();
const readable = new ReadableStream({
  pull(controller) {
    // Never closes, so the stream is still open when it is cancelled.
    controller.enqueue(new Uint8Array(16));
  },
  cancel(reason) {
    onCancel(reason);
  },
});

